 MICHELLE — The problem and purpose | 0:00–0:45

A therapist can hear many important details in a single session. Weeks later, a familiar topic comes up, but finding the exact words and context can mean searching through pages of notes.

MindVault is our Android prototype for local transcription and searchable memory. We want to help therapists find what was said before, while leaving interpretation and clinical judgment with the human.

Today we are demonstrating recording and retrieval using synthetic data.

I’ll hand over to explain what we built around the models.

 ANNA — What we built | 0:45–1:30

The models provide two capabilities: transcription, and embeddings—numerical representations that let us search by similarity.

Our software connects those capabilities into a memory workflow. It divides audio into twenty-second chunks with five seconds of overlap, cleans and merges the transcripts, and stores transcripts and embeddings locally with recording identifiers and time ranges.

When we search, the app ranks stored passages and groups neighboring results. So our contribution is more than a transcript: it is persistent retrieval with a way to inspect the original transcript context.

Let’s show that on the phone.

