 MARTIN — Evidence and limitations | 2:30–3:20

What does our layer add compared with a model alone? Persistent storage, retrieval across recordings, overlap handling, and source time ranges.

We want to distinguish implementation from evidence. The code contains an embedding self-test and six transcript cleanup and merging tests. Those checks are not a benchmark against the plain model, so we are not claiming a measured accuracy improvement yet.

Transcription can make mistakes, and search can return irrelevant passages. Patient isolation, database encryption, and clinical summaries are unfinished in this Android version. That is why we are using synthetic data, rather than presenting this as a clinical deployment.

 MARTIN — First use and closing | 3:20–4:05

On first launch, the user grants permissions and waits for model preparation. The default models require approximately six hundred and forty-five megabytes of downloads.

After preparation, two main actions produce a transcript: Start recording and Stop recording. To retrieve something, open Search, enter a query, and tap Search. We have not yet measured the total first-use time.

Our next evaluation will compare the same synthetic recordings and questions with a defined baseline, measuring retrieval quality and latency on the phone.

MindVault’s goal is simple: retrieve the history, and keep judgment human. Thank you.

